1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,200
Morrow is a private milestone grant system
built on Starknet Mainnet with STRK20.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:10,500
Grant terms stay inspectable, while the public link
to the person receiving the funds is broken.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:16,700
Grant transparency often becomes
wallet surveillance.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:23,300
A normal payout reveals the funder, recipient,
amount, timing, and future wallet activity.

5
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:28,700
Morrow separates accountability from identity.
The terms stay public. The recipient does not.

6
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,300
Before any transaction, Morrow explains
the privacy trade-off.

7
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:40,300
Shield USDC separately, wait for note maturity,
then fund from an existing private balance.

8
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:44,600
Morrow names every public edge—without inventing
a live anonymity score.

9
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,700
Funding is one consequential action.

10
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:55,500
STRK20 withdraws from a private balance
and invokes MorrowEscrow in one action batch.

11
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:01,100
The withdrawal and milestone lock succeed together,
or the entire operation reverts.

12
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,400
Each milestone has two locally generated secrets.

13
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,900
The recipient gets the claim secret.
The operator keeps the recovery secret.

14
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:17,900
They are shown once and acknowledged—
never placed on-chain with a recipient address.

15
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:23,600
The claimant provides the public commitment
and matching secret.

16
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,300
MorrowEscrow verifies it and changes
the milestone state once.

17
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:33,900
STRK20 creates the private output without naming
the owner of the resulting note.

18
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,600
This is not only a prototype story.
Morrow has three helper-mediated Mainnet proofs.

19
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,600
Two transactions funded milestones through
the STRK20 pool and MorrowEscrow.

20
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:51,300
A third claimed a milestone, emitted its event,
and read back as claimed from the contract.

21
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:56,400
The Evidence workspace makes that proof
easy to inspect.

22
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:02,100
Transactions are checked for successful execution
and the expected pool or helper event.

23
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:06,700
Morrow shows the block and links directly
to each public receipt.

24
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:12,100
The interface also handles a real
wallet failure mode.

25
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:17,400
Ready can complete a transaction on-chain
while its browser promise remains pending.

26
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:23,200
Morrow checks the matching event and recovers
the confirmed hash instead of spinning forever.

27
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:28,700
The privacy boundary is deliberately precise.

28
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:34,900
Terms, amounts, deadlines, helper activity,
and timing remain public.

29
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:40,100
Recipient identity, note ownership, and later
private movement stay inside STRK20.

30
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,200
Morrow does not promise perfect anonymity.

31
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,700
Morrow turns milestone funding into
a simple proposition:

32
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:51,600
Fund the work—not the surveillance.

33
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,500
Open source. Starknet Mainnet.
Available now on Railway.
